WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.830
eGPU’s have become more and more popular
in recent years, and many people are now considering

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.370
buying a MacBook Pro with base graphics and
adding an eGPU with something like a Vega

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:15.290
64 or Radeon 7 graphics card instead of buying
a fully specced-out 8-core Vega 20 model.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:20.160
The idea is that you’ll get a portable MacBook
to use on the go while getting a ton of extra

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.180
graphics performance at home with the ability
to upgrade the graphics card down the line.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.400
The concept sounds great, but we’ve discovered
that in a lot of cases, you really don’t

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.410
gain that much performance, and in some cases,
you actually lose performance.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.910
And we’ve also found a way for you to test
your current machine to see if you’ll benefit

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:38.410
from an eGPU or not, so stick around until
the end to find out how.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:45.720
In this video, we tested both the $400 Vega
64 and the $700 Radeon 7 graphics cards using

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.399
the $300 Razer Core X GPU enclosure, which
is in our opinion, the best and most reliable

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:52.410
enclosure you can currently buy for the cash.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.989
We’ll leave links to all of these down in
the description.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:57.700
Let’s get started with Geekbench 4’s graphics
test.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.180
As you can see, the eGPU’s offer a decent
jump in raw graphics performance over the

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.190
Vega 20 graphics in the MacBook Pro, and the
Radeon 7 actually surpasses the performance

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:11.240
of the iMac, which is nice, but it should
be getting a lot more than that, seeing as

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.410
using this card in a Windows PC actually gets
you around 210,000 points.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:21.180
The score is much lower for two reasons, first
of all, eGPU’s send data through Thunderbolt

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.890
3, so there’s a lot of efficiency loss,
and reason number 2 is that even though we’re

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.930
on macOS 10.14.5, the drivers for the card
are still not fully optimized.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.520
Now let’s move onto the Unigine Heaven gaming
benchmark, we actually start to see the Radeon

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.780
7 shine, with over twice as much FPS as the
Vega 20 graphics, so if you’re a gamer,

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:42.880
an eGPU will actually help a lot.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.460
One thing I do want to mention is for the
best results you should always use an external

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:52.000
display plugged directly into the eGPU and
force each program to use it by right-clicking

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.560
on the app in the applications folder, clicking
get info and selecting prefer eGPU.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.729
Now moving onto some Final Cut Pro video editing
tests, we ran the standard BruceX benchmark,

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:08.080
which basically just uses 100% graphics, and
saw a really good performance boost over the

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.980
MacBook Pro by itself, but for some reason,
the Vega 64 actually did just as well.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.480
We’ll explain why we think this is happening
in just a minute.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.030
Now onto stabilizing a 20-second clip, both
eGPU’s perform exactly the same, and only

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.239
a bit faster than the MacBook Pro by itself.

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:28.010
And here is where it gets interesting, this
test is exporting a 5-minute clip of standard

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:33.760
h.264 4K footage, which is basically what
most YouTubers are working with every day.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.720
We actually got much slower times with the
eGPUs, and interestingly, the Vega 64 was

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.510
faster than the Radeon 7.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:42.980
So why the heck is the MacBook Pro faster
by itself?

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.680
Well, this type project is already really
easy and efficient for the standard MacBook

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.940
because of the hardware decoding and encoding
with QuickSync.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.180
Using an eGPU adds a lot of extra steps and
bottlenecks, requiring the data to go from

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.750
the CPU to the thunderbolt controller, through
the Thunderbolt cable and then to the graphics

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.890
card to be rendered, then back again to the
CPU to be encoded instead of doing everything

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.099
inside of the MacBook Pro with no bottlenecks.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:11.190
For the previous tests like Unigine Heaven
and BruceX, the graphics is the bottleneck,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.209
so those scenarios can use an eGPU to its
full potential.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:52.510
Before we move on to some more difficult tasks,
like RED R3D and Canon RAW, I need

00:03:52.510 --> 00:04:06.450
to give a shoutout to our Sponsor, Skillshare.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:11.330
Now some users are starting to move onto HEVC
where the MacBook Pro by itself performs better

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.099
than the Vega 48 iMac!

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:17.809
That’s because it’s packing Apple’s
T2 chip that has hardware HEVC encoding.

00:04:17.809 --> 00:04:22.779
The reason the eGPUs are slower is the same
as before, and once again, the Radeon 7 is

00:04:22.779 --> 00:04:24.360
slightly slower than Vega 64.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.099
Now let’s move onto some more advanced editing,
4.5K R3D RAW, which is heavily dependant on

00:04:29.099 --> 00:04:30.999
the processor and not the graphics.

00:04:30.999 --> 00:04:35.360
Here, we see that the iMac is the fastest
because it has the most powerful processor,

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.059
with the MacBook Pro trailing behind with
or without an eGPU.

00:04:39.059 --> 00:04:43.699
As you can see in this screenshot, the CPU
is completely maxed out and the eGPU is basically

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:47.909
doing nothing because it has to wait on the
CPU to process the RAW footage.

00:04:47.909 --> 00:04:52.220
And now onto C200 RAW footage, which uses
a ton of graphics power, and here, we finally

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:56.590
see the eGPU’s speeding things up, and that’s
because the MacBook Pro by itself was limited

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:58.189
by the Vega 20 graphics.

00:04:58.189 --> 00:05:02.349
In fact, you’ll see in this screenshot that
even the Radeon 7 graphics was the limiting

00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:08.379
factor, since it was 100% maxed out at full
usage while the CPU was chilling at just 39%

00:05:08.379 --> 00:05:09.379
usage.

00:05:09.379 --> 00:05:13.409
And again, the iMac was faster because it
doesn’t have to deal with Thunderbolt 3

00:05:13.409 --> 00:05:14.409
inefficiencies.

00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:17.960
So these were obviously video editing tests,
which don’t use just the graphics, so if

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.610
you’re doing something like gaming or some
other task that really uses tons of graphics

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:25.780
power, you’re gonna see speed boosts similar
to the BruceX and Unigine Heaven results we

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:26.780
got earlier.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.220
But to be honest, the bump in speed isn’t
really what we were expecting, especially

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:35.199
since the Vega 48 should be much slower than
even the Vega 64, but it isn’t because of

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:37.139
eGPU efficiency loss.

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:42.039
Even worse, the Radeon 7 performed basically
the same as the Vega 64, and it costs about

00:05:42.039 --> 00:05:43.460
$300 more than the Vega 64.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.660
This honestly makes us think that Apple took
the easy route of repurposing the Vega 64

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:53.129
drivers for the Radeon 7 since both the benchmarks
and real-world results are so similar while

00:05:53.129 --> 00:05:56.599
the same GPU performs about 50% better in
Windows.

00:05:56.599 --> 00:06:01.000
Hopefully Apple will finally have decent drivers
for it when macOS Catalina comes out in September.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.710
But for now, we recommend just buying the
Vega 64 and saving $300 if you’re gonna

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.179
use it in an eGPU.

00:06:07.179 --> 00:06:12.089
If you’re a video editor using Final Cut
Pro, do not buy an eGPU for your MacBook Pro

00:06:12.089 --> 00:06:16.490
unless you’re editing very specific video
formats like C200 RAW that are very graphics

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:17.490
intensive.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.360
And even then, the performance isn’t that
much better considering you have to deal with

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:24.680
the little glitches and issues that come with
using an eGPU, like your display randomly

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:26.379
going blackout of nowhere.

00:06:26.379 --> 00:06:30.740
Other apps like Resolve and Premiere Pro may
see more gains from an eGPU, and we’re currently

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:34.789
testing that and will post a video on the
Max Yuryev channel, so make sure to subscribe

00:06:34.789 --> 00:06:35.789
there.

00:06:35.789 --> 00:06:39.020
If you’re wondering if you’ll benefit
from using an eGPU, we have a simple test

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:41.669
you can run to find out if you’ll gain performance
or not.

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:46.439
Download iStat Menus 6 from bjango.com and
while you’re doing your specific graphical

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:51.770
work like 3D animation, for example, click
on the CPU icon in the taskbar, and if the

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:56.539
blue processor bar in the graphics card section
is fully maxed out while the CPU is not really

00:06:56.539 --> 00:06:59.319
being used much, that means you’ll benefit
from an eGPU.

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:05.330
If both the CPU and GPU usage are nearly maxed
out, that means that if you buy an eGPU, your

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.860
processor will become the bottleneck and you
might not see much performance gains.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:15.159
But if your CPU is maxed out and your graphics
is not being used much, you won’t gain anything

00:07:15.159 --> 00:07:16.699
at all from an eGPU.

00:07:16.699 --> 00:07:18.430
Now how about for other Macs?

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.909
Should you buy an eGPU for your iMac?

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:21.909
Probably not.

00:07:21.909 --> 00:07:26.340
Because of the Thunderbolt cable inefficiencies,
you probably won’t see much gains at all,

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.360
and you’ll probably lose performance if
you’re using an iMac Pro.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.419
Now Mac Mini users will benefit the most from
an eGPU because it doesn’t come with a dedicated

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:37.520
graphics chip at all, so you kind of have
to get an eGPU for heavy graphics work.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.720
So there you have it, that’s why most people
shouldn’t buy an eGPU for the 2019 MacBook

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:42.720
Pro.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:46.530
If you enjoyed the video, tap like and click
the circle above to subscribe, and make sure

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:47.909
to check out one of those two videos right
there.

00:07:47.909 --> 00:07:53.090
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

